KZ3 patch 2065 (RSX pass 63): an A8R8G8B8 render surface read as Y16_X16_FLOAT gets an R16G16_FLOAT copy of its words
(LD_SURF_RG16=0 = old). rsx_live_draw.c: KZ3's lights read the G-buffer normals target 0x16C0000 (written as RGBA8
bytes, patch 2064) as Y16_X16_FLOAT; the RSX reinterprets the 32-bit word, live-draw bound the RGBA8 UNORM view, so
the normal was two byte values in [0,1]. ld_surface_rg16 runs a compute pass (the depth-snapshot root signature):
word = A<<24|R<<16|G<<8|B, X = half(G<<8|B) (low half), Y = half(A<<8|R), into a per-surface R16G16_FLOAT texture
refreshed when the surface's colour_gen changes; its SRV maps the RSX remap like the guest float-texture path
(ld_float_tex_mapping). Exact for any filter. Log [surf-rg16]. The byte order follows the game's own write
(the G-buffer packs h0.xy = normal.xy and stores the bytes as (y_lo, x_hi, x_lo, y_hi) into R,G,B,A). Note: the
guest-memory Y16_X16_FLOAT decode of patch 2014 maps the FIRST half in memory to X, which this word order says is
Y; KZ3 has no guest Y16_X16_FLOAT texture, so that path is left as it is (open item). Upstream candidate.

